"Relates a customer's service journey to visible staff actions, backstage work and supporting processes.\n\nUse when: Find operational causes of a poor service experience. Coordinate changes across customer-facing and supporting teams.\n\nAvoid when: Only the customer's experience matters or the service's operational scope is unknown.\n\nVisual grammar: Time or journey stages run across columns. Lanes distinguish evidence, customer actions, frontstage, backstage and support. Interaction and visibility boundaries separate fundamentally different service responsibilities.\n\nBuild: Select one actor, scenario and observed service journey. Add the delivery work behind each important touchpoint. Mark failure points, handoffs and operational questions for the responsible teams.\n\nCheck: Backstage work is genuinely invisible to the customer. Support processes connect to actual service delivery needs. Current observations and proposed future arrangements remain distinct.\n\nPitfalls: Adding departmental lanes without preserving the customer perspective. Drawing every internal procedure until the service story disappears.\n\nExample: A hotel arrival blueprint connects guest check-in with reception actions, room readiness checks, cleaning coordination and supporting booking records.\n\nAlternatives: Customer journey map, Swimlane flowchart."
